MAME: hyperscan_card answers only the first 128 bits of any read
================================================================

Found while bringing up src/card.c (2026-08-09). Not applied to the MAME build
used here — src/card.c falls back to 96 single READs when RALL misbehaves, so
the game is correct either way — but the fallback costs 1.26 s where RALL costs
about 0.38 s, and this is why.

The bug
-------
`m_resp_idx` in src/mame/tvgames/hyperscan_card.h is a `uint8_t`. It counts
HALF-bits of the answer the tag is presenting, and it is compared against
`m_resp_buf.size() * 2`:

    if (m_resp_idx <= m_resp_buf.size() * 2)
        m_resp_idx++;

For every command the console's own BIOS issues this is fine, because their
answers are short. It is not fine for RALL, whose answer is

    2 preamble bytes + (HR0, HR1 + 120 data bytes + 2 CRC) * 9 bits = 1114 bits

i.e. 2228 half-bits. The comparison promotes to int and is therefore always
true, so the counter simply wraps at 256 and the answer starts again from its
first bit. A reader clocking the full response gets the first ~12 bytes over
and over; the parity bits still check out, which is what makes it look like a
protocol bug in the reader rather than a counter overflow in the tag.

Nothing in ISO 14443-3 Type 1 limits a response to 128 bits — a real Topaz
streams RALL in full. This is an emulation defect.

The fix
-------
--- a/src/mame/tvgames/hyperscan_card.h
+++ b/src/mame/tvgames/hyperscan_card.h
@@
-	uint8_t              m_resp_idx = 0;
+	uint16_t             m_resp_idx = 0;

`save_item(NAME(m_resp_idx))` needs no change; the state name is unaffected and
save states from before the change are the only casualty.

Verifying it
------------
    python tools/mkcard.py build/card_blank.bin
    make MAIN=src/m19_card.c
    hyprscan.exe hyprscan -rompath "<roms>;build" -memc build/card_blank.bin \
        -quickload build/HYPER.EXE -autoboot_script tools/m19_card.lua \
        -autoboot_delay 0 -nothrottle -video none -seconds_to_run 300

Before: "read 96 user bytes in 1260 ms via 96 single READs (RALL refused)",
        7 RALL fallbacks.
After:  around 380 ms via RALL, 0 fallbacks.

What is NOT worth patching
--------------------------
MAME's WRITE-E completes instantly, where a real tag needs milliseconds of
EEPROM programming per byte. That gap is closed in src/card.c instead
(T_PROGRAM_US), and deliberately so: the delay belongs in the code that will
run on the console, where it is part of the cost the player pays, rather than
in the emulator, where it would be a number nobody ships.
